                "explanation": "<strong>D</strong> <p>When taking the limit of\r                                 a function that is being raised to a power, you can simply raise the\r                                 limit of the function to the same power. This property needs to be\r                                 applied twice in this problem. Remember that taking the square root\r                                 of a function is equivalent to raising the function to the <img src=\"images/0c84517071994cbea9ce09b8bae8f3e7.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\"> power.</p> <p><img src=\"images/b064c5c3e63f4711aa2ac8f6eed6631d.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\"></p> <p>Hence, (D) is correct.\r                                 (B) is the limit of <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>). (C) is the product of 4 and\r                                 2, which is how you handle exponents within a logarithm but not with\r                                 a limit.</p>"
