                "answer": "C",
                "explanation": "<strong>C</strong>  <p>To find the area\r                                     between the curve and the <em>x</em>-axis, use the formula <img src=\"images/07cf10b9d84f4e139fe95fb1006ebf02.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\">, where <em>a</em> and <em>b</em> are values of <em>$\\theta$</em>.\r                                     Here the area is found using <img src=\"images/c31d7bdc419f48579d1dff96c82c3e6f.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\">. The upper and lower bounds are found by the portion of\r                                     the graph shown. Use the calculator to evaluate the\r                                     equation.</p> <p><img src=\"images/9a10f5e5837241cea47eac8a6b26783e.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\"></p> <p>Hence, (C) is\r                                     correct. Another method of using the calculator is to type the\r                                     function into <em>r</em> and then on the home screen type.</p> <p><img src=\"images/2d8cdec35da84646b2ce498d946fba02.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\"></p> <p>Be sure your\r                                     calculator is in polar and radian mode.</p> <p>(A) is obtained if\r                                     you place the squared sign within the integral on only the sine\r                                     function, for example <img src=\"images/44e17bf34bb84ce99f41ea993f602544.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\">. (B) is the result when your calculator is in degree\r                                     mode instead of radian mode. (D) neglects the necessary <img src=\"images/93adc1029ba542e4b4965b4b5d6cc06c.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\"> in the formula.</p>"
