                "answer": "A",
                "explanation": "<strong>A</strong>  <p>You must use your\r                                     calculator to solve this correctly. First, determine the\r                                         <em>y</em>-coordinates of the function. Then find the slope,\r                                         <em>m</em>, of the secant line.</p> <p><img src=\"images/9016052baa9e4f9cafe87f4b7519a022.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\"></p> <p>To find the value(s)\r                                     of <em>c</em> that satisfy the mean value theorem, graph the\r                                     derivative and the function <em>y</em> = 4.118. Then find the\r                                     intersection points. These graphs intersect at 0.295 and 1.846.\r                                     Both of these values are within the domain and satisfy the\r                                     conclusion of the mean value theorem.</p> <p>Therefore, (A) is\r                                     correct. (B) is the result if you find the intersection of 4.118\r                                     with the original function. This is not the conclusion of the\r                                     mean value theorem. (C) and (D) occur if you set the second\r                                     derivative equal to 4.118. You should notice the second solution\r                                     of (D) is not even in the interval [–1, 2] and therefore should\r                                     not be considered.</p>"
